[[:db/retract 17592186220920 :answer-field/choices 17592186220921] [:db/retract 17592186220920 :answer-field/choices 17592186220922] [:db/retract 17592186220920 :answer-field/choices 17592186220923] [:db/retract 17592186220920 :answer-field/choices 17592186220924] {:db/id 17592186221203 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a particular solution has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right) + b_n \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y'_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -{a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{0}=3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{12}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation,\n\n$$\na_0=-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means the constant term is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation,\n$b_n=-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}.$\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{a_n n\\pi}{6}\\right)\\dfrac{n\\pi}{3}-2a_n=\\dfrac{12}{n^2\\pi}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{216}{n^2\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for $b_n$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼-\\frac{3}{2} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{216}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{36}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221204 :question/worked-solution "Because the forcing function has only a constant term and sine terms, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -b_n n^2\\pi^2 \\sin(n\\pi x)\n-\n\\left( \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n \\sin(n\\pi x) \\right)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n(-n^2\\pi^2-1)\\sin(n\\pi x)\n=\n1 + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n^3\\pi} \\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](images/0e/0e33aea74f67de3091fda6d168872458efb0acdba8a4d7b472fea9da1670df27.svg)\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \n-1\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\:\n\\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^3\\pi(n^2\\pi^2+1)}\n\\cdot\n\\sin(n\\pi x).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221206 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation\n\n$$\ny''+9\\pi^2 y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of\n\n$$\n\\omega=\\sqrt{9\\pi^2}=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $\\left(L=1\\right)$ are\n\n$$\n\\omega_n=\\dfrac{n\\pi}{1}=n\\pi.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have $\\omega_3=3\\pi.$ Since $\\omega_3=\\omega,$ the natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function. This is the answer to the first question.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\nf_3(x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=x(A\\cos(3\\pi x)+B\\sin(3\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))+x(-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-6\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)-9{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for $y''_{p_3}$ and $y_{p_3}$ into the equation $y''+9\\pi^2 y=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\left[-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)-9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}\\right]+9\\pi^2 y_{p_3}=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-6\\pi A\\sin(3\\pi x)+6\\pi B\\cos(3\\pi x)=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\sin(3\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n![](images/24/245bf05fb907f9b62a62cda35b50ff309770378a611f4596e023355aec9df276.svg)\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_{p_3}=-\\dfrac{x}{54\\pi}\\cos(3\\pi x).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317443 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-2(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-2{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, ${b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}-2{a}_{n}=\\frac{4}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{72}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186397676 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-5+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-5 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the second equation, ${b}_{n}=\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi }.$ Substituting this into the third equation, gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-5(\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi })=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{15{a}_{n}}{n\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225} \\\\  & =\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{6(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{90(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317452 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=2$, we have ${\\omega }_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{2}=\\omega ,$ the $n=2$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=2$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x)=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″} & =-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{2}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{2}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}]+\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{2}}=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3})=\\frac{5}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4\\pi }{3}A=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4\\pi }{3}B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{15}{16\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{2}}=-\\frac{15x}{16\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222039 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $1$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{4{\\pi }^{2}}=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $1$-periodic function $(L=\\frac{1}{2})$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=2\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))+x(-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+4{\\pi }^{2}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)-4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+4{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-4\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)+4\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4\\pi A=0 \\\\ 4\\pi B=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-223bd29e-2a90-5e8f-a7b8-ad5a6d0bbcc0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186220920 :answer-field/correct "answer-a2d3c588-3230-5c65-b95d-a0c96715b76a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2d3c588-3230-5c65-b95d-a0c96715b76a" "answer-8d164cd9-05f8-521d-a1c1-4640e45ea11c" "answer-ae8c507d-8e09-52ff-853e-45a9bff03a18" "answer-a5bb3694-2f5f-53a7-8495-088462a1c3a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2d3c588-3230-5c65-b95d-a0c96715b76a" :answer/id #uuid "a2d3c588-3230-5c65-b95d-a0c96715b76a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d164cd9-05f8-521d-a1c1-4640e45ea11c" :answer/id #uuid "8d164cd9-05f8-521d-a1c1-4640e45ea11c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae8c507d-8e09-52ff-853e-45a9bff03a18" :answer/id #uuid "ae8c507d-8e09-52ff-853e-45a9bff03a18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5bb3694-2f5f-53a7-8495-088462a1c3a4" :answer/id #uuid "a5bb3694-2f5f-53a7-8495-088462a1c3a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-223bd29e-2a90-5e8f-a7b8-ad5a6d0bbcc0" :answer-field/id #uuid "223bd29e-2a90-5e8f-a7b8-ad5a6d0bbcc0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-19896dd2-dac8-5bfb-aec1-2f10cff206ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8196464-3a2a-5b97-9d21-a30214f0b784" "answer-426d62eb-dc4e-5375-bd07-e202606e3cbb" "answer-19896dd2-dac8-5bfb-aec1-2f10cff206ab" "answer-61b492ca-50cf-52f7-8792-35c237dc1fae"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8196464-3a2a-5b97-9d21-a30214f0b784" :answer/id #uuid "d8196464-3a2a-5b97-9d21-a30214f0b784" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-426d62eb-dc4e-5375-bd07-e202606e3cbb" :answer/id #uuid "426d62eb-dc4e-5375-bd07-e202606e3cbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19896dd2-dac8-5bfb-aec1-2f10cff206ab" :answer/id #uuid "19896dd2-dac8-5bfb-aec1-2f10cff206ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61b492ca-50cf-52f7-8792-35c237dc1fae" :answer/id #uuid "61b492ca-50cf-52f7-8792-35c237dc1fae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{8{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222530 :tutorial/content "Consider the differential equation $y''-4y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause $f$ has no free term and is a pure cosine series, we assume\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y''_p$ and $y_p$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -a_n n^2\\pi^2\\cos(n\\pi x)\n-\n4\\left(\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n\\cos(n\\pi x)\\right)\n=\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty -a_n(n^2\\pi^2+4)\\cos(n\\pi x)\n=\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{2}{n^2}\\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the cosine coefficients, we obtain\n\n$$\n-a_n(n^2\\pi^2+4)=\\dfrac{2}{n^2}\n\\qquad\\Rightarrow\\qquad\na_n=-\\dfrac{2}{n^2(n^2\\pi^2+4)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a Fourier series for a particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\: -\\dfrac{2}{n^2(n^2\\pi^2+4)} \\cdot \\cos(n\\pi x).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221475 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
